# XSH sockatmark()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only the cases that need no out-of-band data: out-of-band data is
# protocol-specific (the page and XSH 2.10.12 do not say how any protocol
# marks the stream, nor when the mark becomes visible), so no assertion
# sends MSG_OOB. The page says 0 is returned when there is no mark, which
# every socket in these tests satisfies.

assertion  XSH.sockatmark.001
anchor     functions/sockatmark.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       sockatmark() on a connected AF_UNIX stream socket with nothing
           queued and no out-of-band data sent returns 0.
test       t/001-no-mark-empty.c

assertion  XSH.sockatmark.002
anchor     functions/sockatmark.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/sockatmark.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       sockatmark() on a connected stream socket with normal data queued
           and no mark returns 0, and does not remove or change the data:
           the data is still received afterwards.
test       t/002-data-no-mark.c

assertion  XSH.sockatmark.003
anchor     functions/sockatmark.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2001
says       sockatmark() with a descriptor that is not open fails with EBADF
           (-1, and a closed descriptor).
test       t/003-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.sockatmark.004
anchor     functions/sockatmark.html#ERRORS/ENOTTY
class      required
since      2001
says       sockatmark() on a descriptor that is not a socket (a pipe end, a
           regular file) fails with ENOTTY.
test       t/004-enotty.c

assertion  XSH.sockatmark.005
anchor     functions/sockatmark.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       sockatmark() on a connected loopback TCP socket with no
           out-of-band data sent returns 0, both with the receive queue
           empty and with normal data queued.
test       t/005-inet-no-mark.c
